added I023, I024, I025, I026, I027, I028; dropped none. The incumbent is E018: 2/3/3 net, Muon at batch 1536, CUDA-graphed step, 9.5 epochs, 0.7571 @ 8.16 s, with about 0.41 pp of margin to spend. The top exploit stacks progressive resizing (5 epochs at 24 px + 6 at 32 px, 11 in total) on this faster, higher-margin base. E015 showed resizing costs about 0.5 pp/s, half the epoch line, so I expect about 0.756 at 7.6 s. The second exploit trims to 9.25 epochs (about 7.95 s, about 0.755). That banks the margin and also measures the epoch exchange rate on the current recipe. Two lower-priority explores tune Muon: an LR floor at 5% of peak (H1.7.5), and batch 1024 with sqrt-scaled lr (extending E017's batch finding). Two more are information-oriented: a group-1 cut to 1 conv with +0.75 epoch, plus a profiler breakdown (H2.4/H2.4.1), and a salted replicate to calibrate perturbation noise (H4.1.1), which many small-delta verdicts depend on. I021 (EMA, H4.2.1) stays queued, which brings the queue to 7. I left H1.2.1 (flip) and H1.3.2 (Cutout) without ideas, because both can only confirm what the incumbent already does and neither would improve time.
